The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
colNameVsField = new HashMap<String, Field>();
colNameVsField.put(field.column, field);
```
Reason:
The original implementation utilized a `Map<String, Field>` which only allows for a single `Field` to be associated with each column name. When multiple fields are linked to the same column name, only the last `Field` added would be stored, ignoring others, resulting in incorrect processing behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
colNameVsField = new HashMap<String, List<Field>>();
List<Field> l = colNameVsField.get(field.column);
l.add(field);
colNameVsField.put(field.column, l);
```
Reason:
The patch modifies `colNameVsField` to be a `Map<String, List<Field>>`, allowing multiple `Field` objects to be stored for each column name. By checking if a list for the column already exists and adding the new `Field` to it, this change ensures that all fields associated with the same column name are correctly processed, thus fixing the bug.

